--- before/docs/specs/studio-observed-authoring/translation-design.md
+++ candidate/docs/specs/studio-observed-authoring/translation-design.md
@@ -63,6 +63,12 @@
 **Accepted static schema epoch (STUDIO-OBS-007).** One `GuidedPromptAssembler` instance owns a complete startup snapshot of the approved output contract, required static schema inputs, all three current provider schemas and their existing provenance hashes. Construction finishes the original reference expansion, shape compaction and hash algorithms before publishing the instance. Missing, malformed, unsupported-reference or canonicalization failures fail construction/startup, including the unconditional bean and retained standalone constructors; no partial snapshot or fallback is published. This deliberately replaces later resource-read failures with startup failures. Schema resource updates require a new instance/application-context restart; development context restart recreates the snapshot. Deployed filesystem/image immutability is not asserted.
 
 The dependency flow is packaged static contract resources → constructor-only schema expansion/provider compaction → private final metadata → a fresh defensive copy from every public provider/provenance getter. `GuidedSourceClassifier` uses the same configured startup assembler; compatible constructors may create one snapshot per classifier instance, never per classification. Exact provider structure, `$defs`/`Shape` ordering, canonical UTF-8, hashes and output bytes remain unchanged. No permissions, current source/configuration rows, owner/fence, projections, model results, credentials, catalog or replay policy enter this snapshot. Every request retains its existing current authority, pin comparison, hash/body, budget, clock, SQL and accounting checks; TRACE requirements and deadlines are unchanged.
+
+**Accepted optional detached projection preparation (STUDIO-OBS-007).** The production assembler/service factory may privately enable a known-pure preparation capability tied to the same assembler epoch used by classification. Public/manual/nonstandard construction keeps the original locked path; no controller/job/configuration flag can select a capability. An extra read transaction repeats fresh authorization/source/owner and the complete original materialization/helper validations, then detaches only the original masked positive model context. Planning-context validation, defensive projection copying and reusable escaped-wire sizing may run off lock. No preparation accounting, recovery normalization, model reservation/send or cached deadline occurs.
+
+Actual reservation retains original fresh guards, accounting and abandoned-attempt settlement, and rereads the owner after nested settlement rather than trusting the old detached wrapper. It repeats full materialization validation and original prompt-input/helper refusals. Only exact fresh context, representation, kind/attempt/repairs/output cap/static metadata and actual recovery-key compatibility allow reuse; any mismatch uses the original locked computation without another request, send or charge. Both original provenance observations remain exclusively at their actual reservation points. Full runner-context/lease equality is never a cached validation certificate: renewal is handled by fresh full validation.
+
+Known fixed typed preparation failures are deferred to their original guarded validation point only when fresh inputs match. Unexpected nonfatal preparation failure makes the optimization unavailable and the original computation decides the outcome; fatal `Error` propagates immediately and is not retained. These extra read/validation/Error observations, possible fallback work and the fresh-after-settlement read are deliberate semantics, not observational equivalence. Full materialization validation still holds the lock in both captures; no one-second performance guarantee is claimed. Backend full TRACE/privacy, current authority, atomic accounting and existing timing bounds remain mandatory.
 
 Transport returns bounded raw response bytes and usage to the guided parser; raw responses remain ephemeral and never enter an audit logger, persisted retry body, public DTO or exception. Guided parser uses the strict wire conventions: one JSON object, no fences/prose, no duplicate keys, trailing content, unknown fields, unsupported version, noncanonical numeric identity or malformed Unicode. Expected session/revision/request kind and allowed coordinate coverage are compared outside schema validation as well. Errors contain codes and safe structural paths, not parser messages quoting raw text. Repair reconstructs the same allowed projection with closed rejection codes and validated opaque IDs; it does not echo the rejected raw response. Legacy compile repair behavior remains unchanged.
 
